{"name":"Safflower Purple","hex":"#b44c97","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"safflower","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cultivated thistle-like plant, Carthamus tinctorius, family Asteraceae, now grown mainly for its oil."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/safflower"},{"term":"purple","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour between red and blue; violet, though often closer to magenta.","Any non-spectral colour on the line of purples on a colour chromaticity diagram or a colour wheel between violet and red."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a purple hue.","Not predominantly red or blue, but having a mixture of Democrat and Republican support."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/purple"}],"entries":[{"title":"Safflower","blocks":[{"text":"Safflower, also false saffron, is a highly branched, herbaceous, thistle-like annual plant in the family Asteraceae. It is one of the world's oldest crops; today, it is commercially cultivated for vegetable oil extracted from the seeds. Plants are 30 to 150 cm tall with globular flower heads having yellow, orange, or red flowers. Each branch will usually have from one to five flower heads containing 15 to 20 seeds per head. Safflower is native to arid environments having seasonal rain. It grows a deep taproot which enables it to thrive in such environments."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Safflower.jpg/330px-Safflower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Safflower"},{"title":"Purple","blocks":[{"text":"Purple is a color similar in appearance to violet light. In the RYB color model historically used in the arts, purple is a secondary color created by combining red and blue pigments. In the CMYK color model used in modern printing, purple is made by combining magenta pigment with cyan pigment, black pigment, or both. In the RGB color model used in computer and television screens, purple is created by mixing red and blue light in order to create colors that appear similar to violet light. According to color theory, purple is considered a cool color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purple"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Illustration_Carthamus_tinctorius0.jpg/500px-Illustration_Carthamus_tinctorius0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Illustration_Carthamus_tinctorius0.jpg/1280px-Illustration_Carthamus_tinctorius0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Illustration_Carthamus_tinctorius0.jpg/500px-Illustration_Carthamus_tinctorius0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Carthamus tinctorius","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIllustration_Carthamus_tinctorius0.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Saffloweroutput.png/500px-Saffloweroutput.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Saffloweroutput.png/1280px-Saffloweroutput.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Saffloweroutput.png/500px-Saffloweroutput.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Worldwide safflower production","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASaffloweroutput.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wiki